  <hr>
  <h2 id="research">Research Data: Skeletal System</h2>
  <p>The human skeletal system is made up of bones, joints, cartilage, ligaments, and other connective tissues. It provides structure, support, and protection to the body, and allows movement. The bones also produce blood cells and store minerals such as calcium. This system is essential for standing, walking, and protecting organs like the brain and lungs.</p>
  <p>More info: <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/spanish/ency/esp_imagepages/9065.htm" target="_blank">MedlinePlus</a></p>
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Human-Skeleton.jpg/800px-Human-Skeleton.jpg" width="100">
  <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="100">

  <hr>
  <h2 id="problems">Problems</h2>
  <p>One major issue is scoliosis, which causes the spine to curve in an "S" or "C" shape. This can cause pain and posture issues. Other skeletal problems include osteoporosis (weak bones that break easily) and kyphosis (hunched back). Our solution is focused on scoliosis and aims to improve posture through a robotic vest.</p>
  <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="100">
  <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="100">

  <hr>
  <h2 id="solution">Robotic Solution</h2>
  <p>Our robot is a wearable vest named <b>SpineGuard</b>. It includes sensors that detect bad posture. When slouching is detected, the vest vibrates gently to remind the user to sit up straight. This can help reduce spinal curvature and train good habits. The vest design was created in Tinkercad.</p>
  <img src="vest_design.jpg" width="100">
  <img src="vest_diagram.png" width="100">

  <hr>
  <h2 id="programming">Programming</h2>
  <p>We used Rogic to create the program for our vest. The program reads a tilt sensor, and if the angle indicates bad posture, it turns on a vibration motor. Otherwise, it stays off.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Algorithm:</b> Detect tilt → Evaluate → If slouching → Vibrate → Else → Wait</li>
    <li><b>Flowchart:</b> Start → Read sensor → Check angle → Yes? → Vibrate → Loop</li>
  </ul>
  <img src="rogic_code.png" width="300">
